{
"id": "lore-distill-a23",
"kind": "lore_distill",
"args": {
"topic": "Tantraloka 23 abhisekaprakasana: consecration of a teacher",
"excerpts": "[Dupuche, The Kula Ritual, ch.29 + consecration]\nOrdinary initiation (samaya-dīkṣā) is essentially a prelude. In TĀ 29 the term dīkṣā properly speaking is reserved to initiation as a Son (putraka), of which there are four types: sabīja (with set ritual and way of life) or nirbīja (without); if he seeks liberation he is called niṣkala.\nThe initiation described in the Ratnamālā involves the mantra KṢRAUṂ and 'oblation' (caru). Under the impact of the mantra placed by the guru on the disciple's hand, the hand moves spontaneously — the movement is called the 'hand of Śiva' (śivahasta) — and hits the body at a spot which indicates the kula to which the disciple belongs, for the body is a hierarchy of śaktis. This blow suddenly causes conviction (pratyaya).\nThe guru should light eight flawless lamps made of gold etc., with red wicks and ghee, worshipped according to the set of eight kula goddesses. Kuleśvara and Kuleśvarī are worshipped in the 'conch-shell' full of the nectar of bliss. By the 'hand of Śiva' the guru consecrates the disciple. Afterwards the Adept should undertake mantra practice.\nThe consecration of the Master: the qualified guru sets out sixty-four 'lamps' made with paste etc., worshipped according to the sixty-four kula goddesses; Kuleśvara and Kuleśvarī in the centre; he consecrates him as guru by the 'hand of Śiva'.\nThe yoginīs are usually sixty-four, interpreted as eight times the eight 'mothers' (mātṛkā); they belong to families (kula) or clans (gotra), with 8 major families (Brāhmī etc.) important at initiation, for the guru ascertains by the manner in which the flower falls to which family and kula the disciple belongs.\nJayaratha reports Abhinava esteemed the guru who follows the Kula ritual procedure (kulaprakriyā) more highly, because of its restfulness, than the guru devoted to tantra ritual procedure.\nThe role of 'Master': one who is an Adept and not consecrated as Master dispenses only supernatural powers; the Master who is a guru grants liberation as well. One may receive consecration (abhiṣeka) from a guru and so become a guru, albeit a 'formed' (kalpita) guru. There is therefore a hierarchy. The recipient of intense descent of energy does not need the external rite of purification, but the rite may be celebrated to manifest the real condition the akalpita guru has already received directly from the goddess.\n\n[Kāmika Āgama]\nFirst and most prestigious of the 28 principal Śaiva Tantras; authoritative for ritual in Tamil Nadu temples; Pūrvabhāga 75 chapters + Uttarabhāga 98 chapters, ~12,000 verses anuṣṭubh. Addresses temple ritual and personal worship, iconography. Śaiva Siddhānta canon, dualist.\n\n[Sanskrit-trikashaivism, TĀ 23 translation excerpts]\nTĀ 23.1-2: \"Now the Supreme Lord's (pārameśvara) procedure (vidhiḥ) of consecration (abhiṣekasya) is declared (kathyate). This (eṣā) which is called initiation of the spiritual son (putraka-dīkṣā) is fit to join (yuktimat) a person to the categories that are objects of enjoyment...\"\nKāmika citation: \"He who does not know in this way the adhva-sandhāna (joining of the paths) is not at all a spiritual preceptor (daiśikaḥ) even if he has been consecrated through the succession starting with samayī.\"\nSiddhayogīśvarīmata citation follows, same point.\nTĀ 23.x (women/śūdra): \"napuṃsakāḥ striyaḥ śūdrā ye cānye'pi tadarthinaḥ | te dīkṣāyāṃ na mīmāṃsyā jñānakāle vicārayet\" — non-men, women, śūdras and others who desire it: they are not to be scrutinized at initiation; one should investigate at the time of knowledge.\nTĀ 23.x: \"Having worshipped and offered oblations like before, and having communicated the intention (cikīrṣitam), the guru should sprinkle (abhiṣiñcet) that disciple sixty-four times (catuḥṣaṣṭyā). Then once with the water full of the sap of those mantras (tad-mantra-rasa-toyena), according to the procedure stated before, with might and greatness (vibhavena), very extensively (suvistīrṇam).\"\nTĀ 23.x: \"Thenceforth that former guru who has abandoned his authority (tyakta-adhikārakaḥ) acts as he wishes regarding explanation, initiation etc., without restriction. He is not bound, because a spiritual lineage (santānaḥ) is like a lamp kindled from a lamp (dīpa-dīpavat). There is no loss of authority, and he is not bound if he does not act. But if he does not perform it before dying, the uninterrupted transmission would be annihilated (vihanyeta).\"\nTĀ 23.x: \"The guru is authorized regarding a group of four tasks (kārya-catuṣṭaye). There should be help for the fallen (patatām) in the form of lending a hand (hasta-ālambaḥ), and not the act of causing them to fall.\"\nTĀ 23.x (sapta-satraka): \"He should protect this group of seven sacrifices: (1) initiation (dīkṣā), (2) explanation (vyākhyā), (3) compassion (kṛpā), (4) friendship (maitrī) ... (7) the giving of food etc. (anna-ādi-dānam).\"\nTĀ 23.22-23: \"During the procedure of consecration the collection of all instruments (sarva-upakaraṇa-vrātam), such as knife, chalk (karaṇī-khaṭikā-ādikam), is to be delivered by the guru to this learned disciple.\"\nTĀ 23.102-103: \"No assignment of adhikāra here, nor indeed the vow of knowledge (vidyā-vrata); what is to be conferred here is the sādhya-mantra in the course of the ritual. All instruction should be the delivery of one's own instruments; the procedure of consecration is declared as the Supreme Lord declared it.\"\nTĀ 23 end colophon: iti kathitam...\n\n[Dyczkowski, TĀ vol.9 ch.16-27 content note]\nVolume nine contains chapters sixteen to twenty-seven. Begins with exposition of initiation into the condition of a teacher's apprentice (putraka) that can lead to consecration as teacher and priest (ācārya). Whereas the basic (samaya) initiation is performed with a single Trident maṇḍala, the power of this higher one is strongly reinforced by worship of three triads and incorporation of the worship of the Twelve Kālīs. Ch.23 is concerned with the consecration of a teacher and ch.24 with an initiate's funerary rites.\n\n[TĀ structure context]\nFirst half ch.1-13 world view; second half ch.14-37 ritual, specifically forms of initiation. Ch.15 ordinary initiation; 16-17 initiation of the Son; 18 shortened form; 19 initiation of the absent; 20 tulā-dīkṣā (scales); 21 initiation of the absent; 22 conversion (liṅgoddhāra); 23 consecration of a teacher; 24 funerary rites; 25 offerings to ancestors; 26 remaining observances; 27 liṅga worship.\n"
