"kind": "lore_distill",
"args": {
"topic": "Tantraloka 17 prameyaprakriya: procedures on the apprentice after initiation",
"excerpts": "[Dyczkowski vol.9 content note]\nChapter seventeen continues with a series of procedures concerning the apprentice: beginning with the fashioning of his sacred thread and the purification of the reality levels he is to traverse in the course of initiation. It continues with the inner illumination of his body with Mantra, the burning away of his fetters, his conjunction to the supreme principle and offerings to the fire.\n[Ch.16] The rest of the initiation involves the complex projection (nyāsa) of layers of Mantras, beginning with those of the Six Paths, onto the neophant's body and the sacrificial area.\n\n[sanskrit-trikashaivism TĀ 17 excerpts]\nChapter 17 = Prameyaprakriyā, 122 verses.\n17.14: \"Now I will speak the procedure (prakriyā) that gives identification with Bhairava (bhāvasyāhammayasvātmatādātmyāveśyamānatā)\" — the procedure by which the state is unified with one's own self as 'I'.\nAbhinavagupta will show how to manufacture Mantras for each tattva/category.\n\"yatra uddiṣṭe vidhau ... tad sādhyam anantaiḥ kriyātmakaiḥ aṃśaiḥ\" — when a procedure has been stated, it is accomplished by endless ritual portions, as in the procedures of initiation; there should be no ūha (substitution/adaptation) there.\n\"pratāḍanam ... astreṇa ... tad-tattva-sthāne ... parimarśayet\" — having struck the disciple with the weapon-Mantra (generally PHAṬ) at the place of that tattva, the guru should touch him.\n\"anye guravaḥ prāhuḥ īdṛśam bhāvanāmayam\" — other gurus said such a procedure consists of contemplation (bhāvanāmaya), not physical ritual.\nnibījā dīkṣā: if seedless initiation is to be performed, the guru throws another full oblation (pūrṇām āhuti) right there.\n17.73: \"eṣa hi śaktipātasya svabhāvaḥ, na pṛthak\" — this is the essential nature of śaktipāta, not something separate.\n[Tantrasara ch.17 correspondence] Liṅgoddhāra (removal of the mark) belongs to ch.22 in TĀ numbering; Tantrasāra's ch.17 covers the same initiatory arc compressed.\n\n[TĀ structure]\nCh.15 ordinary initiation (samaya); 16 putraka initiation; 17 the procedures on the apprentice (thread, tattva purification, mantra illumination, fetter-burning, conjunction, fire offerings); 18 sūkṣmā dīkṣā (11v); 19 utkrānti (death) initiation; 20 tulā; 21 parokṣa (absent); 22 liṅgoddhāra (conversion); 23 abhiṣeka; 24 antyeṣṭi.\n"
},
